{
  "status": "OK",
  "answer_text": "To protect the confidentiality and integrity of transmitted information across internal and external networks, organizations can implement physical or logical measures:\n\n1. Physical protection: Using protected distribution systems (such as wireline or fiber-optics telecommunications systems with adequate electromagnetic, acoustical, electrical, and physical controls) to permit unencrypted transmission.\n2. Logical protection: Employing encryption techniques, such as cryptographic mechanisms (including TLS and IPSec) to protect information from unauthorized disclosure and modification, and cryptographic hash functions (including digital signatures, checksums, and message authentication codes) to protect information integrity.\n3. Compensating controls: Implementing appropriate compensating controls when relying on commercial providers if standard controls and assurances cannot be obtained through contracting.",
  "cited_source_ids": [
    "sc-8_gdn",
    "sc-8.1_gdn"
  ],
  "odp_required_list": []
}

{
